## Development Environment

This project uses [devbox](https://www.jetify.com/devbox) to manage the dev environment.

```bash
devbox shell        # enter the dev environment
devbox run dev      # dotnet watch (port {{ port }})
devbox run test     # xUnit with coverage
devbox run quality  # dotnet format + build with the analyzers
```

Canonical `devbox run` scripts (configured in `devbox.json`):

| Script | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `install` | `dotnet restore --locked-mode` (the `packages.lock.json` files pin every package){% if needs_database %}, after `dotnet tool restore` (dotnet-ef){% endif %} |
| `dev` | the service with reload (`dotnet watch`) |
| `build` | `dotnet build -c Release` |
| `start` | run the service in Release as the image does |
| `test` | `dotnet test` with coverage (coverlet; threshold: 80% lines) |
| `test-fast` | `dotnet test` without coverage |
| `lint` | `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` (whitespace, code style, analyzers; `.editorconfig`) |
| `lint-fix` | `dotnet format` |
| `typecheck` | `dotnet build`: compiler and .NET analyzers, warnings are errors (`Directory.Build.props`) |
| `quality` | `lint` then `typecheck` — the gate CI runs |
{%- if needs_database %}
| `db-up` | a local PostgreSQL matching the defaults in `docs/env-vars.md` (Docker) |
| `migrate-new` | `dotnet ef migrations add <Name>`: the migration for a change of `AppDbContext` |
{%- endif %}
| `smoke` | the tests of category `smoke` against a running deployment: `SMOKE_BASE_URL=https://<host> devbox run smoke` (`test` and `ci` skip them) |
| `audit` | trivy against the locked NuGet packages (fails on HIGH or CRITICAL) |
| `secrets-scan` | detect-secrets against `.secrets.baseline.json` |
| `security` | `audit` + `secrets-scan` |
| `secrets-baseline` | refresh `.secrets.baseline.json` with fixed flags (never run `detect-secrets scan` by hand) |
| `ci` | `install` + `quality` + `test` + `security`: the gates CI runs, then `ci-extra` when your repo defines it; run it before every push |
| `image-build` | Build local image `{{ project_name }}:scan` |
| `image-scan` | trivy against `{{ project_name }}:scan` |

**Rule for every agent (human, CI, and AI): never call `dotnet`,{% if needs_database %} `dotnet ef`,{% endif %} `detect-secrets`, or `trivy` directly. Always go through `devbox run <script>` (add a missing recipe to `devbox.json` rather than bypassing it). A new package: add its `<PackageVersion>` to `Directory.Packages.props` and a version-less `<PackageReference>` to the project, then `devbox run -- dotnet restore` and commit the changed `packages.lock.json`.**

## Claude Code agents

Agents and slash commands come from the [`ika100/sdlc-foundry`](https://github.com/ika100/sdlc-foundry) marketplace, enabled in `.claude/settings.json`:

- `svc-dotnet@sdlc-foundry` — .NET service coder, tester, deployment, observability, release agents
- `svc@sdlc-foundry` — product-manager, architect and the other pipeline agents
- `component@sdlc-foundry` — the commands: `/component:spec`, `plan`, `build`, `ship`, `fix`, `check`, `release`
- `instance@sdlc-foundry`, `api@sdlc-foundry`, `issue@sdlc-foundry`, `platform@sdlc-foundry` — `/instance:promote`, `/api:list`, `/issue:report`, `/platform:update`
- `shared@sdlc-foundry` — quality and security agents, the `cplat` launcher

### Common workflows

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Write a feature spec (first step) | `/component:spec <description>` |
| Plan the approved spec | `/component:plan <NNN>` |
| Build it: tests first, verified | `/component:build <NNN>` |
| All of it unattended: build, PR, merge on green CI, dev | `/component:ship <NNN>` (headless: `cplat headless "/component:ship <NNN>"`) |
| Where specs stand | `/component:spec --all` |
| Small change | `/component:fix <description>` |
| Read-only quality + security audit | `/component:check` |
| Release | `/component:release` |

### Spec first

A feature starts with a spec, not code ([ADR-026](https://github.com/ika100/sdlc-foundry/blob/main/docs/adr/026-feature-specs.md)): `/component:spec <description>` writes `docs/specs/<NNN>-<slug>/spec.md` with acceptance criteria `AC-<NNN>.<n>` and asks you its open questions; approve it (`/component:spec approve <NNN>`), plan it (`/component:plan <NNN>`), then build it (`/component:build <NNN>`): acceptance tests are written from the criteria first and fail, the code makes them pass, a reviewer checks the result against the spec, and the PR lists every criterion. Tests that name a criterion are the spec in code: never weaken them; change the spec instead (`/component:spec --amend <NNN> <change>`). `/component:spec --all` shows where each spec stands; `devbox run spec-check` validates every spec and traces built ones to their tests (also the warn-only `specs` CI workflow). Small changes and bug fixes (`/component:fix`) need no spec, but stop when they would change a criterion.

## Feature Branch Workflow

Branch prefixes: `feature/`, `fix/`, `chore/`, `docs/`, `refactor/`, `release/`. Slugs: lowercase, hyphens, ≤40 chars. Main is protected; all changes via PR. Required CI checks: `quality`, `test`, `security`, `pr-title`, `docker`. PR titles follow Conventional Commits (`type(scope): description ≤72 chars`).

