## Conventions

- **Layout.** `src/{{ namespace }}/ServiceApp.cs` builds the app (logging, probes, routes) and `Program.cs` runs it; `tests/{{ namespace }}.Tests/` holds the xUnit tests. Group routes by feature in `MapGroup` extension methods (`app.MapOrders()`) called from `ServiceApp.Build`.
- **C#.** Nullable reference types on, warnings are errors; never silence an analyzer without a justifying comment next to the suppression.
- **Validation:** check every input of a route (typed parameters, a request record, `Results.ValidationProblem`) and answer 400 before the handler does work.
- **Config:** read exclusively from environment variables in `AppConfig.cs` (document each one in `docs/env-vars.md`).
- **Probes:** `/health` (liveness) and `/ready` (readiness) must keep responding; register a health check tagged `ServiceApp.ReadyTag` for every dependency the service needs.
- **Logging:** inject `ILogger<T>` and log through `[LoggerMessage]` methods: one JSON line each (`JsonLineFormatter`){% if needs_observability %}, carrying `trace_id` and `span_id` inside an active span{% endif %}. Never log secrets or whole request bodies.
{%- if needs_observability %}
- **Observability:** `/metrics` (Prometheus via prometheus-net: .NET and process metrics, `http_requests_total`, `http_request_duration_seconds` in `HttpMetrics.cs`); OpenTelemetry tracing in `Telemetry.cs`, exporting only when `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` is set.
{%- endif %}
{%- if needs_database %}
- **Database:** PostgreSQL through EF Core (`src/{{ namespace }}/Data/`). The schema is `AppDbContext`; every change gets a migration from `devbox run migrate-new <Name>` in `Data/Migrations/` (commit it; never edit a released migration). The service applies pending migrations at startup. Connection settings are the `PG*` variables the platform's postgres resource injects (`docs/env-vars.md`); locally `devbox run db-up` starts a matching PostgreSQL. Integration tests use Testcontainers (`DatabaseTests.cs`, Docker required). Readiness includes the database.
{%- endif %}
- **Tests:** xUnit v3 in `tests/{{ namespace }}.Tests/`; call the service in memory through `TestApp.Create()` (`WebApplicationFactory`) instead of opening a port, and pass `TestContext.Current.CancellationToken`. Smoke tests live in `Smoke/` (see Smoke tests).
- **Docs:** feature specs in `docs/specs/` (index in `docs/backlog.md`), ADRs in `docs/adr/`.

## Smoke tests

`devbox run smoke` runs the smoke tests against a deployed instance (`SMOKE_BASE_URL`); the platform runs it after every deploy to dev and staging and on `/instance:verify <env>`. It also sets `SMOKE_ENV` (`dev`, `staging`, `prod`) and, outside dev, `SMOKE_READ_ONLY=1`: then tests of category `writes` are not run, so staging and prod never get smoke data.

- A smoke test is an xUnit test with `[Trait("Category", "smoke")]` that skips without a base URL (`Assert.SkipWhen(string.IsNullOrEmpty(baseUrl), ...)`, `tests/{{ namespace }}.Tests/Smoke/HealthSmokeTests.cs`).
- A smoke test reaches the service only through the base URL (`HttpClient`), creates its own uniquely named data (`smoke-<guid>`) and never changes or deletes data it did not create.
- Mark every smoke test that creates, changes or deletes data as writing: `[Trait("Category", "writes")]` next to the smoke trait. It runs only in dev.
- Keep at least one read-only smoke check (reads, health, lists) for every behaviour you want checked in staging and prod. Outside dev, a run in which no smoke test passed is reported as **not run** (no read-only smoke test), never as passed.

## Docker image pipeline

CI's `docker` job builds the multi-stage, non-root, chiseled image on every run and pushes on `main` (`latest`, `sha-<40>`, `sha-<short>`); a `v*.*.*` tag gives the tested `sha-<40>` image its semver tags (`release-image`). Registry: `{{ docker_registry }}/{{ project_name }}`, authenticated with `GITHUB_TOKEN`.

The image is the chiseled ASP.NET Core base image (about 125 MB unpacked, 57 MB to pull) plus the published service, without `web.config` or the API documentation file. Keep it framework-dependent and untrimmed: trimming the runtime breaks EF Core and reflection-based code at run time (`PublishTrimmed` reports over 100 trim warnings for this service).

## Deployment

This repo ships **only a container image**. Kubernetes manifests, environment wiring (e.g. `API_URL`), replicas, resources and exposure belong to the system's `gitops-app` repo (platform ADR-017): register the component with `/component:add {{ project_name }}` in the system's repo (needs the GitHub topic `deployable-service`, set by `/component:new`), then `/instance:promote {{ project_name }} --to <env>`. Change how the service runs by editing its entry under `components:` in that repo's `system.yaml`; change the port, probe paths or user in the Dockerfile/app and update the entry to match.

